ign="center">0.84&#x02013;2.44</td></tr><tr><td align="left">D &#x02265; 3</td><td align="center">0.62</td><td align="center">0.14&#x02013;2.86</td><td align="center">0.84</td><td align="center">0.23&#x02013;3.04</td><td align="center">0.74</td><td align="center">0.28&#x02013;1.97</td><td align="center">1.10</td><td align="center">0.51&#x02013;2.33</td></tr><tr><td align="center" colspan="9"><bold>Psychotropic drug use</bold></td></tr><tr><td align="left">D = 1</td><td align="center">1.04</td><td align="center">0.77&#x02013;1.40</td><td align="center">1.13</td><td align="center">0.85&#x02013;1.52</td><td align="center">1.08</td><td align="center">0.88&#x02013;1.33</td><td align="center">1.16</td><td align="center">0.97&#x02013;1.38</td></tr><tr><td align="left">D = 2</td><td align="center">1.20</td><td align="center">0.85&#x02013;1.69</td><td align="center">1.53*</td><td align="center">1.09&#x02013;2.14</td><td align="center">1.35*</td><td align="center">1.06&#x02013;1.72</td><td align="center">1.36&#x02020;</td><td align="center">1.11&#x02013;1.66</td></tr><tr><td align="left">D &#x02265; 3</td><td align="center">1.44</td><td align="center">0.91&#x02013;2.28</td><td align="center">2.23&#x02021;</td><td align="center">1.43&#x02013;3.46</td><td align="center">1.81&#x02021;</td><td align="center">1.32&#x02013;2.48</td><td align="center">1.77&#x02021;</td><td align="center">1.36&#x02013;2.30</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>*P &#x0003c; 0.05, &#x02020;P &#x0003c; 0.01, &#x02021;P &#x0003c; 0.001.</p><p><sup><italic>a </italic></sup>Students were excluded (n = 643). <sup><italic>b </italic></sup>Odds ratios adjusted for age.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T5"><label>Table 5</label><caption><p>Relationships between deprivation score (D)and tobacco, excessive alcohol, and frequent psychotropic drug use for various age groups among retired people: odds ratios and 95% CI, vs. D = 0</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="gro